  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p><strong>Setting up an AWS Lambda function</strong> (Option 1) is the correct approach, as Lambda lets you encapsulate the custom rule logic necessary for your complex compliance requirements. <strong>Creating a custom rule in AWS Config and attaching the Lambda function's ARN</strong> (Option 2) ensures AWS Config can automatically invoke your custom logic to evaluate resource compliance.<br/><br/>Other options are less suitable:<br/><ul><li><i>Launching a t2.micro EC2 instance</i> (Option 3) is unnecessarily complex and would require manual management and monitoring, which is not scalable compared to using a serverless Lambda function.</li><li><i>Configuring an SNS topic and subscribing via email</i> (Option 4) is useful for notifications but does not perform compliance checks or enforce security requirements.</li><li><i>Creating a custom rule in AWS Config that runs every hour and sends messages to SQS</i> (Option 5) only moves the issue to a queue; it doesn't perform the actual compliance logic that's needed.</li></ul><br/><strong>Tip:</strong> For advanced compliance scenarios not handled by AWS managed rules, always use Config custom rules with Lambda, as this allows you to codify and automate any required checks specific to your environment.</p>",
  "query": "<p>Your company enforces strict security standards and wants to proactively check AWS resources for possible vulnerabilities. The security team has asked for an automated solution within AWS Config to verify that all AWS resources stay compliant with specific security policies. Existing AWS managed rules cannot cover the complexity of these policies. <br/>Which combination of actions will enable a custom compliance check for AWS resources in this scenario? (Select TWO options.)</p>",
